{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And he shall uncover the woman's head.</b> He shall uncover, and in my opinion the root פרע [means] he shall remove [the hair of] her head that was covered." "<b>Waters of bitterness.</b> In my opinion, the word מי is in construct state and the word המרים is an attributive adjective of the noun. If so, its sod is known. Also possible is that they are named for their outcome, like \"and you shall strip off the clothes of the naked\" — and the proof is \"and the water that brings a curse shall enter her to become bitter,\" to indicate that the imprecations called המאררים make the waters bitter, after they had been sweet." "And the word <b>המאררים</b> is from the intensive [binyan], like \"which YHWH has cursed.\" (Genesis 3:14)"], :book :numbers, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {2 "The verse cited is בראשית ג' י\"ד; the lemma reads אשר אררה ה' but the Masoretic text has אררה אתה, suggesting Ibn Ezra is quoting from memory or a variant tradition."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:16:28.878537540Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}